othe","H7181":"to listen","H1697":"word","H8267":"deception","H8334":"to minister","H7326":"be poor","H8501":"deceitful","H6298":"to meet","H215":"to light","H5869":"eye","H8147":"two","H3068":"LORD","H571":"faithful","H3678":"throne","H5703":"perpetuity","H3559":"prepare","H7626":"staff","H5414":"give","H5288":"youth","H7971":"depart","H954":"be ashamed","H517":"mother","H4658":"carcass","H7200":"see","H3256":"to discipline","H1121":"child","H5117":"to rest","H4574":"delicacy","H2377":"vision","H6544":"to lead","H8104":"obey","H8451":"instruction","H835":"blessed","H5650":"servant/slave","H3588":"for","H4617":"answer","H2372":"to see","H213":"to hasten","H8615":"cord","H4480":"from","H6445":"to pamper","H5290":"youth","H319":"end","H1961":"to be","H4497":"progeny","H1624":"to stir up","H4066":"strife","H1167":"master","H2534":"rage","H7227":"many","H1346":"pride","H120":"man","H8213":"to abase","H8217":"low","H8551":"to grasp","H3519":"glory","H5973":"with","H1590":"thief","H423":"oath","H8085":"hear","H5046":"to tell","H2731":"trembling","H982":"to trust","H7682":"to exalt","H6440":"before","H8441":"abomination","H5766":"injustice","H1870":"conduct"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Corrupt. Hebrew, “scoffers,” who provoke both God and men."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Rest; or bring him to hear reason. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “a wise man shall rule nations; but the wicked being angry, is laughed at, and does not frighten.”"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Soul. They wish to protect the upright, Psalm cxli. 5."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Enlightener. Septuagint, “visitor,” with punishment, as they seem to speak of palliated usury, which cannot escape God. See chap. xxii. 2."],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Prophecy, by the urim, or by the mouth of prophets, who were in great power and estimation, 3 Kings i. 24., and xii. 23., and 2 Paralipomenon xxv. 6., and xxviii. 8. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “There shall be none to explain the law to the impious nation.” (Haydock) — When pastors are wanting, all goes to ruin, 1 Corinthians xi. 4. (Ven. Bede) (Calmet)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Amendment. St. Jerome (ad Evag.) says, after Thucydides: “Ignorance produces confidence, and learning fear.”"],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Soul, and exposeth himself to death, though this was only inflicted for stealing a man, Exodus xxi. 16. The night-thief might also be killed in the fact, Exodus xxii. 2. But if the person who had stolen denied it on oath, he was put to death for perjury, Leviticus v. 1."],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Fall. Human respects will not long preserve him from sin. (Calmet) — Septuagint have a double version: “Those who fear and are ashamed of men, shall be thrown down. But he who confideth in the Lord, shall rejoice. Impiety overturneth man, while he who trusteth in the Lord shall be saved.” (Grabe) (Haydock)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[11,null,0,"The Book of Pastoral Rule","Part 3",0,604,"Let the impatient hear what is said again through Solomon; A fool uttereth all his mind, but a wise man putteth it off, and reserves it until afterwards. For one is so driven by the impulse of impatience as to utter forth the whole mind, which the perturbation within throws out the more quickly for this reason, that no discipline of wisdom fences it round. But the wise man puts it off, and reserves it till afterwards. For, when injured, he desires not to avenge himself at the present time, because in his tolerance he even wishes that men should be spared; but yet he is not ignorant that all things are righteously avenged at the last judgment."],[11,null,1,"Conferences of John Cassian - Part II","16. The First Conference of Abbot Joseph",1,435,"WE ought then to restrain every movement of anger and moderate it under the direction of discretion, that we may not by blind rage be hurried into that which is condemned by Solomon: \"The wicked man expends all his anger, but the wise man dispenses it bit by bit,\" i.e., a fool is inflamed by the passion of his anger to avenge himself; but a wise man, by the ripeness of his counsel and moderation little by little diminishes it, and gets rid of it."]],"fau":[["gregory-great","Gregory the Great",1,"father",null,1,604],["cassian","John Cassian",0,"father",null,0,435]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 12 (1895)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf212"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 11 (1894)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf211"]]}